  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery and RAP analysis currently highlight troughing \nover the eastern CONUS as a mid/upper ridge continues to build over \nthe Desert Southwest. At the surface, high pressure situated over \nthe Plains continues to shift to the east. As a result, light winds \nobserved throughout the forecast area will gradually turn to \nthe east and southeast through the remainder of today. \nFurthermore, mostly clear skies and a modestly dry airmass will \nallow for seasonably cool conditions overnight into Monday \nmorning, with forecast lows in the middle 60s areawide.\n\nThe aforementioned upper ridge will slide eastward and promote the \nreturn of seasonably hot afternoon temperatures across a majority of \nthe forecast area to begin the week. For Monday, locations\ngenerally along and west of I-135 will see highs ranging from \nthe middle 90s to near 100 degrees, while areas to the east will\nsee one more day of afternoon temperatures near 90 degrees. \nAdditionally, a tightening pressure gradient developing in \nresponse to lee troughing across the High Plains will result in \nbreezy south winds (gusts up to 25-35 mph) across portions of \nsouth central and especially central Kansas on Monday afternoon.\nFor Tuesday, a slight southward shift in the ridge will result \nin slightly warmer temperatures (upper 90s) across southern \nKansas, while locations to the north will see more seasonable \ntemperatures near the mid 90s. Guidance has remained consistent\nin maintaining dewpoints in the upper 50s and low 60s through \nthis period, which should keep heat indices fairly close to dry-\nbulb temperatures. \n\nModel consensus continues to highlight a potent shortwave traversing \nthe Northern Plains over the next several days which will serve to \ngradually displace the aforementioned ridge southward. As a result, \na slow-moving cold front will trek to the south and east across the \nPlains. Repeated ripples in midlevel flow along with this surface \nfeature will promote repeated chances for periodic showers and \nstorms across the forecast area beginning Tuesday and lasting \nthrough the end of the week. To this point, coverage appears rather \nisolated on Tuesday with better chances arriving Wednesday morning \nand lasting through Friday. Strong to marginally severe storms may \nbe possible Wednesday afternoon and evening amid moderate \ninstability and modest shear, though where these develop (if at all) \nmay depend on small-scale features that are not quite evident at \nthis time.\n\nTemperatures during the mid to late week period will likely depend \non the extent of rain and cloud coverage as the surface boundary \nprogresses to the southeast. For now, highs in the upper 90s are \nforecast across southern Kansas on Wednesday before a return to\nthe middle 90s on Thursday. Readings closer to 90 and perhaps \neven lower are expected across areas to the north over the same\ntimeframe.",
